I think this is occasionally a necessary distinction to make and we should 
have some terminology for doing so (I'm less sure it is really a problem 
here).  The dictionary seems to favor the text version:  it is explicitly a 
text that is the predicate relation {bridi},  though {selbri} might be taken 
to be the relation itself rather than the predicate that expresses it.  
Similarly, {sumti} is said to be the argument (ambiguous) of a predicate 
(text) or function (object) filling place number (text)/type (object?). We 
don't have -- in either Lojban or the technical English of Lojbanitry a good 
det of words to distinguish them.  For now, let's use l-sumti (from {la'e}) 
for the things and s-sumti ({sa'e}) for the words.  So, {mi e do} is one 
s-sumti but stands for two l-sumti -- or more. (Question: does {mi'o} stand 
for one l-sumti or two -- or more?) Well, {le mlatu} is an s-sumti and stands 
for a/some l-sumti.

